  "text": "1. α (I → RA → I, same subject), with a stated lever (I → lever port) and an undercut (I → CA → RA)\nAlice: The neighbours are home. Their lights are on. Lights on mean someone is home.\nBob: They leave their lights on when they travel.\n\n2. α with no PTA lever (FV): lever \"novel\"\nAlice: Mia will win the race. She is the best runner.\n\n3. α on second-order statements: both about the same proposition (◎), neither marked A/U\nBob: Nothing is certain.\nAlice: That is false. It refutes itself.\n\n4. β (I → RA → I, same predicate): scopes give the substance\nAlice: Socrates is mortal. All men are mortal.\n\n5. γ i (I → RA → I, nothing shared): the lever settles i\nAlice: Peace can be started by the truth, because war can be started by lies.\n\n6. γ d (nothing shared, no relation): lever \"none\"\nAlice: The moon is made of cheese. My cat is orange.\n\n7. No form (same subject and predicate): the premise restates the conclusion\nAlice: Cats are mammals. Cats are mammals!\n\n8. δ I (second-order I premise about the conclusion, perspective I): conclusion q itself, implicitly A\nAlice: The meeting is at nine. I booked the room myself.\n\n9. δ II (second-order I premise, perspective II) into a second-order conclusion marked U\nBob: Vaccines are dangerous.\nAlice: That is not to be believed. You sell supplements.\n\n10. δ III (second-order I premise, perspective III)\nAlice: Smoking causes cancer. Every oncologist says so.\n\n11. δ II from a locution (L → RA): the addressee's own words; perspective from the TA's speakers\nBob: Exercise is good for you.\nAlice: Then you are committed to that.\n\n12. δ III from a reported locution (L → Asserting → L(reported) → RA): conclusion q itself\nAlice: The ferry is late. Carol said so.\n\n13. No form from a locution that does not say the conclusion: argue from its I-node instead\nAlice: Take an umbrella. Carol said it will rain.",
  "dialog": true
}